## A B S T R A C T
Herein, we report the preparation of an organic–inorganic hybrid hydrogel architecture using vinyl alginate and UiO-66 MOFs (metal-organic frameworks) modified with acrylic acid (AA) UiO-66AA. UiO-66 MOFs with different crystal sizes (600, 1500, and $2500\mathrm{nm}\cdot$ ) were synthesized and the effect on the mechanical and transport properties of the resulting materials, such as water absorption capacity and drug release, were evaluated. HydroMOF showed higher water absorption capacity than the pure hydrogel and enhanced mechanical prop­ erties, which depend on crystal size and the amount of UiO-66AA MOF used. The initial release rate of drug (burst release) from hydroMOFs was lower when small-sized crystals or a small amount of large-sized crystals were used; thus these are essential in changing half-life values of release rates. Finally, the cytotoxicity screening successfully showed that hydroMOFs are promising biocompatible compounds proven to have the advantages of minimized burst release and mechanical robustness.
## 1. Introduction
Hydrogels are water-based three-dimensional networks constructed from physically and/or chemically cross-linked polymers (Wu, Chen, & Li, 2017). Due to the presence of hydrophilic groups (e.g., $-\mathrm{OH}$ , $\begin{array}{r}{-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2},}\end{array}$ −COOH, and $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left.-\mathrm{CONH-CONH_{2}}\aftergroup\egroup\right.$ in the polymer chain, hydrogels can be prepared to possess a persistent shape, which confers upon them the ability to absorb large amounts of water without losing their original form. Hydrogels are described as soft materials because of their hydro­ philicity and low stiffness (Hamedi, Moradi, Hudson, & Tonelli, 2018). Biocompatibility is one of the most important properties of hydrogels, making them useful in a wide variety of biomedical applications (e.g., wound dressings, tissue engineering, and drug delivery systems) (Cal´o & Khutoryanskiy, 2015; Giri, Bhowmick, Pal, & Bandyopadhyay, 2011).
Natural polymers (e.g., chitosan, collagen, gelatin, hyaluronic acid, and alginate) have been extensively studied for the preparation of hydrogels. Sodium alginate (SA) is a water-soluble polysaccharide extracted from brown seaweed, which consists of a linear copolymer of
1–4 linked $\scriptstyle({\mathfrak{X}}-\mathrm{L}$ -guluronic (G), and $\upbeta$ -D-mannuronic (M) acids. Alginate exhibits a wide variety of properties such as biocompatibility, biode­ gradability, non-toxicity, and non-immunogenicity (Du et al., 2017; Pogrebnjak et al., 2019). Hydrogels have the advantage of being less toxic and more biocompatible in vivo, but demonstrate poor mechanical properties that may cause abrupt rupture during the swelling process (Zhang, Zhang, & Wu, 2013).
Although hydrogels possess a series of attributes, the low mechanical strength caused by high amounts of absorbed water restricts their ap­ plications. The brittle nature of these materials is a serious drawback that has been an ongoing research problem for over half a century. The use of composite technology and hybrid materials has emerged and shows great potential toward the preparation of resilient hydrogels. From a technical point of view, hybrid and composite materials are essentially different, which are better understood when analyzing the relationship between their structure and properties. Composites are obtained by mixing two or more constituents whose individual proper­ ties are integrated to exhibit new physical and chemical properties. The concept of hybrid materials goes beyond a simple physical combination of organic and inorganic phases and is the result of coupling individual molecules on a molecular level that can imprint new and adjustable properties into new architectures (Du et al., 2017).
Several inorganic materials have been used in a continuous effort to create hydrogels with enhanced mechanical properties, including mes­ oporous silica (de Lima et al., 2018), montmorillonite (Su & Chen, 2018a, 2018b), graphene oxide (Zhuang et al., 2016), Zinc oxide (ZnO) nanoparticles (Wang et al., 2019), and laponite (Mahdavinia, Mousa­ nezhad, Hosseinzadeh, Darvishi, & Sabzi, 2016). Metal-organic frame­ works (MOFs) are an emerging class of inorganic–organic crystalline porous materials, which are built from cluster nodes or metal ions and organic linkers (Furukawa, Cordova, O’Keeffe, & Yaghi, 2013; Yaghi et al., 2003). The University of Oslo (UiO) family of MOFs based on zirconium show high thermal, chemical, and mechanical stability (DeCoste, Demasky, Katz, Farha, & Hupp, 2015). UiO-66 has been applied in various fields, including gas adsorption (Niu et al., 2018), supercapacitors (Tan, Zhang, Gao, Wu, & Tang, 2015), fluorescence sensors (Yang et al., 2015), and drug delivery (Mocniak, Kubajewska, Spillane, Williams, & Morris, 2015). Using modulators with similar chemical functionalities as organic linkers, the crystal size of UiO-66 can be controlled (Schaate et al., 2011). Furthermore, solvent-assisted ligand incorporation (SALI) can increase the functionality of MOFs (Deria, Bury, Hupp, & Farha, 2014). Due to these characteristics and non-toxicity (Zhu et al., 2014), UiO-66 is an ideal candidate for hybrid hydrogels.
Hybrid materials based on inorganic substances and hydrogels have attracted a lot of research interest from contemporary investigators. Upon loading inorganic materials into a hydrogel, the hybrid material has an increased elastic modulus than that of the pristine polymer (Du et al., 2017). Apart from the enhancement in their mechanical properties in drug delivery applications, these inorganic materials also help control drug release (Siangsanoh, Ummartyotin, Sathirakul, Rojanapanthu, & Treesuppharat, 2018).
Compared with conventional administration systems, controlled release devices show superior therapeutic effects which result in the reduction of dosage frequency (Liu et al., 2020). Although hydrogels are attractive for use in such devices, they show a significant burst effect due to a high initial drug release rate, reducing the period of action in vivo. The burst effect can be minimized or eliminated by creating a transient barrier with the addition of nanoparticles into a polymeric network, preventing the fast drug diffusion from the hydrogel. (Hezaveh & Muhamad, 2012; Xu et al., 2014).
While the use of inorganic species to improve polysaccharide-based material properties has been explored, the effects of the quantities and crystal size of particles in the drug delivery mechanism and mechanical properties need further investigation. In this context, the aim of this study was to synthesize resilient hybrid hydrogels using alginate and UiO-66 MOF nanoparticles (hydroMOF) for drug delivery with attenu­ ated burst release. The influence of the quantity and crystal size of UiO66 MOFs on the mechanical and drug transport properties of the resulting hydrogel was studied using UiO-66 MOFs with different crystal sizes (600, 1500, and $2500\mathrm{nm}$ ). Prednisolone was used as a drug model due to its wide therapeutic applications, including the treatment of ul­ cers, rheumatism, asthma, arthritis, and multiple sclerosis. The cyto­ toxicity of the materials was then evaluated to verify biocompatibility.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
$N,N.$ -Dimethylformamide (DMF) $(99\%)$ , hydrochloric acid (HCl) (37 $\%)$ and ethanol $(99~\%)$ were supplied by Synth. Glycidyl methacrylate (GMA $97\ \%,$ ), 2-hydroxyethyl methacrylate (HEMA, $97\:\:\%,$ ), $N_{\star}^{\prime}N_{\star}^{\prime}$ - dimethylacrylamide (DMAAm, $97~\%$ , sodium persulfate (NaPS) $(\geq98$ $\%)$ , $N,N,N^{\prime},N^{\prime}$ -Tetramethyl ethylenediamine (TEMED), Zirconium(IV)
chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ , terephthalic acid (BDC), acrylic acid (AA) $(97\ \%)$ , prednisolone $(\geq99~\%)$ , and sodium alginate (SA) $\mathsf{50-60~k D a}$ ; Man­ nuronate/Guluronate ratio $=1.42$ ) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. SA shows an average viscosimetric molar mass $\left(\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{v}}\right)$ of $73.07\mathrm{{kDa}}$ and intrinsic viscosity (η) of 0.1285 P. The viscometry measurements were carried out at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $0.1\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ NaCl aqueous solutions. The value of $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{v}}$ was obtained using the Mark–Houwink–Sakurada parameters: $a=$ 0.97 and $K=2.0\times10^{-3}\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Zheng et al., 1998).
2.2. Synthesis of UiO-66 MOFs with crystal sizes of 600, 1500, and 2500 nm
The preparation of UiO-66 MOFs with controllable crystal sizes was achieved using an acid/base approach, previously reported in Yang et al., 2017. Different amounts of triethylamine $(70\mathrm{~-~}1115~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , 0.6 mmol of BDC, $0.06\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{zrCl}_{4}$ and $20.60~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetic acid were heated at reflux in $140~\mathrm{mL}$ of (N,N-dimethylformamide) DMF at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 6 h (Table S1 in the supporting information (SI)). The resulting so­ lution was cooled to $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the UiO-66 MOF products were collected via centrifugation at $2000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ . The materials were washed thrice using DMF and methanol and then stored at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ prior to use. The materials were named MOF UiO-66-2500, MOF UiO-66-1500, and MOF UiO-66-600.
## 2.3. SALI of AA in UiO-66
MOF UiO-66 was activated upon heating at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for $24\mathrm{h}$ before carrying out the SALI reaction. In this process, $0.1\mathrm{~g~}(0.061$ mmol) of MOF UiO-66 was loaded into a glass flask containing $10\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and $0.61\mathrm{mmol}$ of AA. The flask was closed and heated in an oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ with occasional swirling. After the SALI reaction, the material was collected via centrifugation at $2000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ , washed thrice with DMF and methanol, and finally dried under vacuum at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The materials were named MOF UiO-66-AA2500, MOF UiO-66-AA1500, and MOF UiO-66-AA600.
## 2.4. Functionalization of SA with glycidyl methacrylate (GMA)
$1.0~\mathrm{g}$ of SA was added to $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of water until a homogenized solu­ tion was obtained. HCl $\left(2.0\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\right)$ was added dropwise until the pH of the solution was adjusted to 3.5. Then, $0.65\mathrm{mL}$ of GMA was added to the reaction mixture and heated at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Acetone was used to precipitate the vinyl SA product. The precipitate was filtered under vacuum and then freeze-dried for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . This material was named SAGMA (vinyl SA).
## 2.5. Preparation of the hydrogel and hydroMOF materials
Here, $0.25\:g$ of vinyl SA, $0.75{\mathrm{~g~}}$ of HEMA, and 0.25 of DMAAm were dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of water at room temperature with continuous stir­ ring. Different amounts of the MOF UiO-66, functionalized with AA, were added to obtain 0.1 or $0.5~\%$ (w/w) solutions of UiO-66AA with different crystal sizes (see Table S2 in the SI). An aqueous suspension was formed and $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of SPS and $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of TEMED were added. The resulting hybrid materials (HydroMOF) were washed with Milli- $Q^{\bigotimes}$ water and then labeled as 600HydroMOF, 1500hydroMOF, and 2500hydroMOF, with the UiO-66AA content labeled as either 0.1 or 0.5 $\%$ . The characterizations of the materials, including the detailed pro­ cedure of water transport, are described in the SI.
## 2.6. In vitro drug release
Prior to gelation, prednisolone $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ with respect to the total mass of the polymer) was introduced to the hydrogel and hydroMOF forming mixture during the synthesis. The drug-loaded dry hydrogels of known weight were immersed into $250~\mathrm{mL}$ phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) at
$37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a pH of 7.4 and stirred at $100\mathrm{rpm}$ . At different times, $3.0\mathrm{mL}$ aliquots were collected and the absorption was measured at $247~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which is the wavelength for the maximum absorption of prednisolone, on an ultraviolet (UV)-Vis spectrophotometer (Varian Cary 50 UV–vis spectrophotometer). All aliquots were returned to prevent volume loss. The prednisolone released from the materials was determined from the analytical curves correlating the absorption to the concentration of prednisolone.
## 2.7. Cytotoxicity assay
VERO-lineage cells were cultivated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a $5\%$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at­ mosphere in Dulbecco’s modified eagle medium (DMEM; Gibco, MO, USA) containing $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (Invitrogen, Sao Paulo, Brazil) and $1\%$ penicillin/streptomycin (Gibco). After reaching $80\%$ confluence in a cell-culture flask (TPP, Trasadingen, Switzerland), the cells were suspended in a solution containing $25\ \%$ trypsin–ethylenediamine tet­ raacetic acid (Gibco). The cell concentration was adjusted to $2\times10^{5}$ cells/mL in DMEM without penicillin/streptomycin and the suspension was added to 96-well plates (TPP). Prior to cytotoxicity testing, the wells were washed twice using PBS and the cells were exposed to 1H (hydrogel), 2H $0.1\ \%$ MOF UiO-66), or 3H $(0.5~\%$ MOF UiO-66) at concentrations ranging from 1 to $1000~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~{\mathrm{mL}}^{-1}$ . Untreated cells were used as positive controls (Fig. 1). The test was performed after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a $5\%$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ atmosphere. Cytotoxicity was measured using the reduction of 3-(4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-5-(3-car­ boxymethoxyphenyl)-2-(4-sulfophenyl)-2H tetrazolium (Promega, WI, USA) in DMEM without phenol red. After $3\mathrm{h}$ of incubation in the dark, the absorbance of formazan (the product of the bioreduction of VERO cells soluble in a tissue-culture medium) was determined at $490~\mathrm{{nm}}$ using an ASYS instrument (Biochrom, MA, USA). The percentage cell viability $(\%$ CV) was calculated using the following formula: ${\%}\mathrm{CV=}$ $(\mathrm{A_{f}}/\mathrm{A_{c}})\times100$ , where $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{t}}$ and $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{c}}$ refer to the absorbance of the test sub­ stance and control (untreated cells), respectively (Malich, Markovic, & Winder, 1997). The cytotoxicity of the compounds was evaluated as the mean of three independent experiments.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. MOF UiO-66 synthesis and AA SALI functionalization reaction
Our strategic investigation of the crystal size effects on the physical hydrogel properties and burst drug release phenomena start with the synthesis of UiO-66 MOFs with controllable crystal sizes. Analysis of the experimental and simulated PXRD diffraction peaks obtained for the MOF UiO-66 samples confirmed that the MOF UiO-66 material was obtained (Fig. S1). The size of the crystals (approximately 600, 1500, and $2500\mathrm{nm}\cdot$ ) were determined by dynamic scattering light (DLS) after the SALI reaction (Fig. S2) and the values are listed in Table S3.
The hybrid materials are prepared so that the inorganic materials can form covalent bonds with the polymer network. Thus, any further incorporated materials must have some functional group to chemically cross-link with the main macromolecular chain. Therefore, AA was attached to the MOF UiO-66 crystal structure using the SALI function­ alization reaction (DeCoste et al., 2015). The FTIR spectra recorded for the MOF UiO-66 samples functionalized with AA are shown in Fig. 2(a). The new prominent band observed at $1658~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in the materials after the reaction was attributed to the vinyl groups $\mathtt{C}\mathrm{=}\mathtt{C})$ originating from AA, which demonstrated that the AA molecules were inserted into the MOF UiO-66 structure (Ward, Schofield, Badyal, Goodwin, & Merlin, 2003). The PXRD diffraction results shown in Fig. 2(b) demonstrate that the MOF UiO-66 crystal structure was preserved during the SALI pro­ cedure, which also confirmed that the AA SALI reaction was successful. The SALI reaction is a typical acid-base reaction that proceeds with the displacement of two water molecules from the nodes of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{6}$ . This is possible because our material is not a defect-free MOF. Thus, there are some missing links in the synthesized MOF structure (Deria et al., 2014). Fig. 2(c) summarizes and demon­ strates the SALI reaction on the zirconium MOF.
## 3.2. Characterization of the alginate and functionalization
SA is organized in consecutive $\mathrm{~L~}$ -guluronic acid (G blocks), consec­ utive D-mannuronic acid (M blocks), or alternating G and M blocks. The ratio between the M and G blocks gives valuable insights into the gelling properties of the alginate (Sakugawa, Ikeda, Takemura, & Ono, 2004). An approach based on FTIR spectroscopy previously reported (Filippov & Kohn, 1974) was used to determine the M/G ratio. The intensity of specific bands (Fig. 3(a)) for the mannuronic blocks $(1125\mathrm{cm}^{-1}).$ and for the guluronic blocks $\cdot1030\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) were analyzed and the M/G ratio was determined to be 1.42. The bands were identified and associated with the alginate structure. The bands at 3500 and $2930~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were associ­ ated with OH stretching and $\mathrm{C-H}$ vibrations, respectively. The high-intensity bands at 1600 and $1400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were associated with asymmetric and symmetric COO- stretching vibrations of carboxylate groups, respectively. The bands at 1035 and $813~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were associated with C–OC group stretching vibrations and C––H deformation vibra­ tions of mannuronic acid residues, respectively (Shaari & Kamarudin, 2020). Two new bands were observed after the GMA functionalization reaction. The bands at 1746 and $1710~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ observed in the GA spec­ trum correspond to the carbonyl groups $\left(\nu{\mathsf{C O}}\right)$ and carbon–carbon double bonds $\scriptstyle(=\nu\mathsf{C}=\mathbf{C})$ in the conjugated system, respectively (Guil­ herme et al., 2005).
The $^1\mathrm{H}$ -NMR spectra recorder for SA and SA-GMA shown in Fig. 3(b) are in agreement with the FTIR spectra. The SA spectra exhibited two anomeric protons at 5.05 and $4.66~\mathrm{ppm}$ , associated with the H of guluronic acid and the H of mannuronic acid, respectively (Cong, Xiao, Liao, Dong, & Ding, 2014). After the GMA functionalization reaction, two new signals appearing at $\delta=6.18$ and $5.66~\mathrm{ppm}$ in the SA-GMA spectrum were assigned to the hydrogen atoms linked to the vinyl car­ bon atoms. The signal observed at $\delta=1.8$ ppm can be attributed to the hydrogen atoms of the methyl groups linked to the vinyl carbon atoms. The integration of these signals displays a 1:1:3 ratio, which is in good agreement with the two hydrogen atoms in the terminal vinyl group and three hydrogens for the methyl group (Reis et al., 2009). The reaction scheme shown in Fig. 3(c) summarizes the SA functionalization reaction.
## 3.3. Hydrogel synthesis
The hydrogel and hydroMOF samples were synthesized from the cross-linkable SA-GMA, HEMA, and DMAAm using MOF UiO-66-AA as an inorganic cross-linker in the presence of SPS as the initiator and TEMED as the catalyst. The proposed reaction mechanism for the syn­ thesis of the hydrogel and hydroMOF samples is illustrated in Fig. 4. The
MOF UiO-66 SALI functionalization reaction with AA, using chaingrowth polymerization chemistry, yielded cross-linkable inorganic ma­ terial. Typically, APS was left to react to produce sulfate anion-radicals, which can capture the vinyl groups, leading to the growth of the grafted polymer chain via a radical addition reaction.
## 3.4. Swelling behavior of the hydrogels and hydroMOFs
The swelling degree (SD) of a material gives important insight into water transport through the hydrogels, which can be directly related to their water absorption capacities. These materials are not only valued for their mechanical properties, but also for their water absorption po­ tentials; materials with greater SD values have a wider range of appli­ cations. The swelling mechanism of a hydrogel involves transport phenomenon. For example, the movement of water occurs from a region of high activity to a region of low activity due to a decrease in the Gibbs energy, indicating that it is a spontaneous process. Before hydration, the activity of water is greater outside than inside the hydrogel, which rapidly changes the driving force. The swollen-dry boundary of the polymer network accommodates the absorbed water in the bulk mate­ rial. The leading cause of this can be attributed to the poreinterconnected amorphous flexible structure containing functional groups, which ensures favorable water diffusion into the system. When the activities of water outside and within the hydrogel are balanced, the system achieves a swelling equilibrium.
Considering the driving forces for the change in the swelling prop­ erties in response to external stimuli, the samples were swollen in buffer solutions at $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ , 5.0, and 3.5, at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (SI). These values were selected considering variations in the pH of the gastrointestinal tract. Table 1 shows the SD of the samples (Equation S1) and the influence of the amount and crystal size of the UiO-66 MOF used. The hydroMOF samples exhibit a higher SD than that of the hydrogel at pH 7.4. This SD increase for hydroMOF materials can reach at least $239\%$ for 2500hydroMOF- $.0.1\ \%$ and up to $300\%$ for 2500hydroMOF- $.0.5~\%$ . However, the water absorption capacity of the hydroMOF samples at pH 5.0 and 3.5 decreased by $1-14\ \%$ and $5\mathrm{-}10~\%$ , respectively, suggesting pH-responsive characteristics. The increase in SD at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ is associated with the −COOH groups in UiO-66 (aqua and one hydroxo) $(\mathrm{{pKa}_{1}=}$ 3.51; $\mathsf{p K a}_{2}=4.82\$ (Shearer et al., 2016) and the −COOH in alginate, which can be ionized to form negatively charged $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ groups. This has particular relevance to factors that directly affect the swelling properties, such as hydration and hydrogen bonding. Furthermore, the charged groups cause electrostatic repulsive forces that increase the distance between the polymeric chains, which makes the movement of water into the hydrogel easier (Su & Chen, 2018a, 2018b).
The semi-empiric Korsmeyer–Peppas model (Equation S2) was used to understand how water was transported through the hydrogels and how the swelling mechanism changes with time. The diffusion coeffi­ cient (n) is characterized by the mechanism of water diffusion with values of 0.45 and 0.89 observed for Fickian diffusion and case-II transport, respectively, and $0.45<\mathtt{n}<0.89$ indicates the existence of an intermediary state between diffusion and relaxation. Pseudo-Fickian diffusion behavior is observed at n $<0.45$ (Siepmann & Peppas, 2001). The values of n observed for the hydrogel and hydroMOF samples were $<0.45$ , suggesting a pseudo-Fickian water diffusion mechanism, which is observed when the time needed to achieve swelling equilibrium is very long, as reported by (Singh & Kumar, 2018) (Table 1).
Table 1 shows the swelling kinetics constant (k) values obtained using Equation S3 for the hydrogels and hydroMOF samples. In a buffer solution at $\mathrm{pH}5.0$ and 3.5, the samples rapidly swell, whereas, at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ , the samples swelled slowly.
## 3.5. Morphological properties
The structures of the freeze-dried hydrogel and hydroMOF samples swollen to equilibrium in a buffer solution at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ were studied using SEM, as shown in Fig. S3. The hydrogel and hydroMOF materials exhibit an interconnected porous amorphous structure, indicating that the presence of the inorganic species does not change the morphological structure of hydrogel and that either UiO-66 MOF content or crystal size modify the amorphous phase. Such a phase is less dense in the polymer chains, which form a less dense cross-linked network. This type of ar­ chitecture can allow the greater movement of water and solutes.
## 3.6. Mechanical properties
Table 2 shows the results obtained for the mechanical properties of the hydrogel and hydroMOF materials including the elasticity moduli (E), resilience $(\mathrm{U}_{\mathrm{r}})$ , and toughness (T). For comparison purposes, the variation in the E values $(\Delta\mathrm{E})$ was also calculated for the hybrid mate­ rials with respect to the pure hydrogel.
The resilience $\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathrm{U}_{\mathrm{r}})$ and toughness (T) of hydroMOF materials were recorded to be higher than those of pure hydrogel. The data of 2500hydroMOF- $.0.1\%$ (Table 2) shows that the inclusion of MOFs in the polymer backbone increases the (Ur) and T by approximately 1118 and $1022\%$ , respectively. The size of UiO-66 MOF crystals at $2500\ \mathrm{nm}$ and lower increased the elasticity moduli by up to $193\%$ (2500hydroMOF$0.1\mathrm{~\%~}$ .
The elastic moduli of the hydroMOF samples depend not only on the crystal size but also the amount of MOF UiO-66AA used. This becomes evident by ordering the hybrids from most relevant to least relevant as follows: 2500hydroMOF- $0.1\ \%>\ 1500$ hydroMOF- $.0.5\ \%>1500$ hydro­ MOF-0.1 $\%>$ 600hydroMOF-0.5 $\%$ $>$ 2500hydroMOF-0.5 $\%>$ 600hydroMOF- $0.1\ \%$ . Taking in account the crystal size, the order was 2500hydroMOF- $.0.1\ \%>2500$ hydroMOF- $0.5~\%$ for MOF UiO-66-2500; 1500hydroMOF- $0.5\%>1500\$ hydroMOF- $0.1\ \%$ for MOF UiO-66-1500; and 600hydroMOF- $0.5\ \%>600$ hydroMOF- $0.1\ \%$ for MOF UiO-1500.
Unlike the 1500hydroMOF and 600hydroMOF samples, the me­ chanical strength of the 2500hydroMOF samples were not significantly improved upon increasing the amount of MOF UiO-66-2500 used. The performance of these materials, when compared with those of other hybrids, is related to the larger size of crystals which are not easily dispersed inside the polymer network due to their tendency to agglomerate, yielding heterogeneous hybrids with lower elasticity moduli.
To sum up, when compared with the hydrogel, the hydroMOF sam­ ples exhibit an increase in all the parameters studied, suggesting they form a more robust polymeric network. In general terms, the increase in these parameters can be related to the enhanced mechanical charac­ teristics of the hybrid architecture whose organic and inorganic phases are bonded to each other via covalent cross-linking (Li, Wang, Zhang, Xu, & Liu, 2018).
## 3.7. In vitro drug release
## 3.7.1. Burst release effect
Fig. 5a, c, and e show the cumulative release curves obtained for prednisolone in the hydrogel and hydroMOF samples at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ . The initial burst release was studied by evaluating the first $60\mathrm{min}$ of release $\left(\mathbf{t}_{60}\right)$ (Fig. 5b, d, and f; Table 3). The $\mathbf{t}_{60}$ values obtained for the hydro­ MOF samples, with the exception of 2500hydroMOF- $.0.5~\%$ , were an order of magnitude lower than that of the hydrogel sample, suggesting attenuated burst release. This requires a discussion on the effects of the crystal size and amount of MOF UiO-66 on the hydrogel properties, such as those observed for the mechanical properties.
The release rate was found to be decreased when using small-sized crystals or small amount of large-sized crystals. The $\mathbf{t}_{60}$ values of 2500hydroMOF- $.0.1\%$ , 600hydroMOF- $.0.1\%$ , and 600hydroMOF- $.0.5\%$ were, $37\ \%$ , $42\%$ and $70\%$ lower than that of pure hydrogel, respec­ tively. In these cases, the crystals are well dispersed inside of the structures and create tortuous troughs that retard drug release toward the outside of the hybrid material.
The atypical behavior of 2500hydroMOF- $0.5\%$ was attributed to the crystal size and amount of MOF UiO-66 used, which can agglomerate and form void spaces (void volume) that make drug release into sur­ rounding liquid more favorable.
## 3.7.2. Release mechanism
To make a better assessment of the release process, the experimental release kinetics data obtained for prednisolone were fitted using four mathematical models, which describe the non-steady state diffusion of molecules moving through a liquid medium: (i) Power law (Equation 2), (ii) zero-order kinetics (Equation S4), (iii) first-order kinetics model (Equation S5), and (iv) second-order kinetics models (Equation S4).
The n values were determined from the slope of the logarithmical curve obtained for ${M}_{t}/{M}_{\infty}$ as a function of time. The power law equation was applied to a cumulative release of up to $60\%$ . The release kinetics for prednisolone were determined using the zero-order kinetics model, first-order kinetics model, and second-order kinetics model, applying non-linear least-squares fittings. The best fits of the non-linear models to the experimental data are shown in Fig. S4 and the fitting parameters are shown in Table 3.
The hydrogel and hydroMOF samples show an anomalous mecha­ nism for prednisolone release $(0.45<\mathtt{n}<0.89)$ , indicating the existence of an intermediary state between diffusion and macromolecular relax­ ation. In this case, the release of prednisolone from the hydrogel, which is time-dependent, disturbs the arrangement of the polymer network, and acquires a new conformation. The n values are slightly higher for the hydroMOF samples than those of the hydrogel, which suggests that the presence of MOF UiO-66 affects the release mechanism, once the SD of the hybrid material was higher than that of the pure hydrogel. The same transport behavior was observed in a hybrid hydrogel of poly(acryl­ amide) and diatomite $(\mathrm{Lv},$ Sun, Jin, & Jiang, 2019).
The experimental release data were better correlated with the firstorder and second-order models, taking into account that the $\mathrm{\ttR}^{2}$ values were 0.98. The good fit of both models requires an analysis of the con­ centration and chemical nature of the solute being released.
The first-order kinetics refer to the initial dissolution of a poorly hydrophilic guest molecule inside the swollen hydrogel, which is applicable to prednisolone and shows that it moves via an anomalous mechanism, in accordance to the swelling data (W´ojcik-Pastuszka et al., 2019). The second-order model describes a concentration-dependent mechanism for prednisolone. In addition, the values obtained for the rate constants $(\mathsf{K}_{1}$ and ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{\cdot}}$ ) were low, suggesting that the release of prednisolone took a considerable amount of time to reach an equilib­ rium state. These assumptions were supported upon the analysis of the half-life of prednisolone release $(\mathrm{t}_{1/2})$ , which is the time necessary to release $50\%$ of the total loading amount of the drug inside the hydrogel. On the whole, the hybrid materials show half-life values of several hours or longer than those of the pure hydrogel. This implies that the $\mathbf{t}_{1/2}$ value responds to changes in the hydrogel structure, which can be used to control the release of drugs when the burst release decreases with an increase in the half-life.
## 3.8. Cytotoxicity assay
Vero cells presented approximately $95\ \%$ viability after $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ of exposure to higher concentrations of the compounds used for cytotox­ icity testing $(1000\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ (Table S4). At lower concentrations, the cell viability was higher, indicating that the compound had no in vitro cytotoxicity (data not shown). No significant differences were found between the treated and untreated groups.
The hydroMOFs were not cytotoxic, which is most likely due to the compatibility of alginate and UiO-66 (Aba´nades La´zaro et al., 2018; Das, Pham, Lee, & Noh, 2019). Researchers have concentrated their efforts on developing inert compounds that can be used in medical applications. Our cytotoxicity screening was successful as it revealed that hydroMOF material is a promising new compound to use for efficient drug delivery without any toxicity.
## 4. Conclusions
HydroMOF hydrogel materials were synthesized using vinyl alginate and the zirconium UiO-66 MOF functionalized with acrylic acid AA by SALI (UiO-66AA). The presence of UiO-66AA inside the hydrogel enhanced the elasticity moduli (E), resilience $(\mathrm{U_{r}})$ , and toughness (T) by up to $193\ \%$ , $1118~\%$ and $1022~\%$ , respectively. Furthermore, the prednisolone release demonstrated that the insertion of an MOF in the hydrogel can reduce burst release, depending on UiO-66 MOF crystal size and content. The hydroMOFs are not cytotoxic, which is the result of the compatibility of alginate and UiO-66.
